Power BI Desktop Basics & Workflow

🔹 Objective
Understand the foundational workflow of Power BI Desktop, including data loading, transformation, and basic visualization creation.

🔹 Power BI Desktop Overview
Power BI Desktop consists of three primary working views:

1️⃣ Report View
Used to create and design visualizations.
Allows building interactive dashboards using charts and filters.

2️⃣ Data View
Displays raw table data.
Used to inspect column values and verify dataset integrity.

3️⃣ Model View
Used to define and manage relationships between tables.
Critical for maintaining correct aggregations.

🔹 Data Import Process

Learned how to:
Import structured datasets (e.g., Excel files).
Load data into Power BI.
Understanding the dataset structure is essential before creating visuals.

🔹 Data Transformation (Power Query)

Used Transform Data to open Power Query Editor.

Key observations:
Transformations appear under Applied Steps.
Each step records changes made to the dataset.
Changes are only applied after selecting Close & Apply.
Power Query ensures data is cleaned before analysis.

🔹 Applied Steps Concept

Every transformation is tracked sequentially.
Steps can be edited or removed.
This creates a reproducible data-cleaning workflow.
This reinforces the importance of structured preprocessing before visualization.

